//@version=5
strategy("T58 EMA Ribbon RSI Reversal")

// New strategy (2026-08-31), deliberately a different entry SHAPE from
// the three existing PineScript files in this folder:
//   - trend_pullback.pine: 2-EMA pullback with a static RSI band
//     (price simply has to be inside a range).
//   - ema_momentum_breakout.pine / rsi_mean_reversion.pine: their own
//     separate mechanisms.
//   - This one uses a slower mid/slow EMA pair as the regime filter --
//     deliberately not the fast EMA, since a fast EMA reacts to the
//     same short-term pullback that pushes RSI toward oversold/
//     overbought, which would flip a fast-EMA-based regime off at
//     exactly the moment the entry trigger fires -- combined with an
//     RSI THRESHOLD-CROSS EVENT (crossing up through oversold / down
//     through overbought) as the entry trigger, rather than a static
//     "RSI is currently inside a band" condition. A crossover is a
//     specific moment (the reversal just happened), which fires far
//     less often but with a cleaner signal than "RSI happens to
//     currently be in this range." The fast EMA still earns its place,
//     just in the exit instead of the entry gate: losing the fast EMA
//     against the position means the pullback that was bought/sold
//     failed to reclaim.
//
// Same T58 PineScript parser subset restriction as every other
// strategy here: only ta.sma/ema/wma/rsi, ta.crossover/crossunder,
// input.int/float constants, and strategy.entry/close. No custom
// functions, no ta.atr/highest/lowest, no security() (see
// app/strategy/pinescript.py for the exact supported list).
//
// SL/TP sizing: gold-scale (pip_size ~0.01), NOT the FX default of
// 0.0001 -- click "detect pip size from data" on the Data tab before
// running this. The other pinescript/mql5 files in this repo originally
// used FX-style 15-40 "pip" stops, which are $0.20-0.40 at gold scale --
// nowhere near gold's per-bar ATR -- and stopped out almost instantly
// (1-2 trades, catastrophic loss) the one time they were actually run
// against real gold data. The 650/1300 values below are a starting
// point at gold scale, not a validated setting.
//
// NOT YET VALIDATED: run through Full Pipeline before trusting any
// constant below -- these are GA starting points.

fastLen = input.int(9, "Fast EMA")
midLen = input.int(21, "Mid EMA")
slowLen = input.int(55, "Slow EMA")
rsiLen = input.int(14, "RSI Length")
rsiOversold = input.float(35.0, "RSI Oversold Level")
rsiOverbought = input.float(65.0, "RSI Overbought Level")

fastEMA = ta.ema(close, fastLen)
midEMA = ta.ema(close, midLen)
slowEMA = ta.ema(close, slowLen)
rsiVal = ta.rsi(close, rsiLen)

// Trend regime uses only the mid/slow pair -- deliberately NOT the fast
// EMA, since the fast EMA reacts to the same short-term pullback that
// pushes RSI toward oversold/overbought, which would flip the regime
// off at exactly the moment the entry trigger fires. Mid/slow stays
// stable through that pullback, so the regime filter and the entry
// trigger measure genuinely different things instead of fighting each
// other.
uptrend = midEMA > slowEMA
downtrend = midEMA < slowEMA

rsiReclaimLong = ta.crossover(rsiVal, rsiOversold)
rsiReclaimShort = ta.crossunder(rsiVal, rsiOverbought)

longCondition = uptrend and rsiReclaimLong and close > open
shortCondition = downtrend and rsiReclaimShort and close < open

// The fast EMA earns its place in the exit instead: once price loses
// the fast EMA against the position, the pullback that was bought/sold
// has failed to reclaim, which is a cleaner invalidation than waiting
// for the slower mid/slow trend filter itself to flip.
exitLongCondition = ta.crossunder(fastEMA, midEMA)
exitShortCondition = ta.crossover(fastEMA, midEMA)

strategy.entry("Long", strategy.long, when=longCondition)
strategy.entry("Short", strategy.short, when=shortCondition)

strategy.close("Long", when=exitLongCondition)
strategy.close("Short", when=exitShortCondition)

// T58_SL_PIPS=650
// T58_TP_PIPS=1300
